{
  "fromId": "n",
  "toId": "r_flap",
  "preamble": "Rhotacism of the alveolar nasal: /n/ loses its nasal airflow and becomes the alveolar tap [ɾ]. There are three main contexts. Between vowels: Tosk Albanian against Gheg, and Istro-Romanian and some northern Romanian dialects in words of Latin origin. After a stop or nasal in an initial cluster: northern Irish and Scottish Gaelic kn, gn, mn > kr, gr, mr, often with a nasalized vowel after it. By dissimilation in the Old Spanish cluster -mn-: omne > omre, later hombre.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Rhotacism, Denasalization, Dissimilation",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Tosk Albanian",
      "languageFamily": "Albanian",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Gheg gjuni 'the knee'",
          "to": "Tosk gjuri [ˈɟuɾi]",
          "note": "Intervocalic n became the tap r [ɾ] in Tosk; Gheg keeps n"
        },
        {
          "from": "Gheg zâni 'the voice'",
          "to": "Tosk zëri",
          "note": "Same change; also druni vs druri 'the wood', Shqypnia vs Shqipëria 'Albania'"
        },
        {
          "from": "Gheg pluhun 'dust'",
          "to": "Tosk pluhur",
          "note": "Same change"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Istro-Romanian",
      "languageFamily": "Eastern Romance",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Latin bonus 'good' (cf. Daco-Romanian bun)",
          "to": "bur /bur/",
          "note": "Intervocalic n became r in words of Latin origin"
        },
        {
          "from": "Latin bene 'well' (cf. Daco-Romanian bine)",
          "to": "bire /bire/",
          "note": "Same change"
        },
        {
          "from": "Latin canis, canem 'dog' (cf. Daco-Romanian câine)",
          "to": "câre /kəre/",
          "note": "Same change"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Irish",
      "languageFamily": "Goidelic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "cnoc /kn̪ˠuk/ 'hill'",
          "to": "Ulster /kɾˠɞk/",
          "note": "/n/ after a stop becomes a tap in northern dialects; southern dialects keep /kn/"
        },
        {
          "from": "mná /mˠn̪ˠɑː/ 'women'",
          "to": "Ulster /mˠɾˠãː/",
          "note": "After /m/, with the following vowel nasalized"
        },
        {
          "from": "gnó /ɡn̪ˠoː/ 'business'",
          "to": "Connacht /ɡɾˠoː/",
          "note": "After /ɡ/"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Scottish Gaelic",
      "languageFamily": "Goidelic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Old Irish cnocc 'hill'",
          "to": "cnoc /kʰɾɔ̃xk/",
          "note": "The cluster /kn/ became /kɾ/, with nasalization moved onto the vowel"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Spanish",
      "languageFamily": "West Iberian",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Old Spanish omne 'man' (< Latin hominem)",
          "to": "omre",
          "note": "Dissimilation of /n/ after /m/; an epenthetic -b- later arose in /mɾ/, giving hombre /ˈombɾe/"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
